Two translation decisions worth knowing about:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mprintf[0m is called through [38;5;10mextern "C"[0m rather than reimplemented with Rust formatting. The format string is fixed and only interpolates two [38;5;10mint[0ms, so Rust's [38;5;10mprint![0m would emit the same characters — but it would use a separate, independently-flushed buffer. If a caller interleaves its own [38;5;10mprintf[0m with calls into this library, that difference shows up as reordered lines. Borrowing libc's [38;5;10mprintf[0m keeps the buffer shared.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
C's [38;5;10mdiv()[0m truncates toward zero with the remainder taking the numerator's sign, which is exactly Rust's [38;5;10m/[0m and [38;5;10m%[0m on integers, so a plain [38;5;10mDivT { quot: numer / denom, rem: numer % denom }[0m matches. The two inputs C leaves undefined — [38;5;10mdenom == 0[0m and [38;5;10mINT_MIN / -1[0m — are deliberately not "fixed". On x86-64 the C raises [38;5;10mSIGFPE[0m; Rust's division aborts. Both die before reaching [38;5;10mprintf[0m, so stdout is empty either way, though the signal/exit code differs. I verified the well-defined cases by diffing against the gcc-built shared library; I did not verify the two UB cases, since there is no defined C behavior to compare against.[0m[0m
